new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e1240f7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/data-transfers.md
@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+# Exporting and importing Fizzy data
+
+Account admins can export the entire account from **Account Settings**. Anyone can
+export their accessible cards from their own profile. Both run in the background
+and email the requester a ZIP download link valid for 24 hours. The link requires
+the requester's signed-in identity and account access; account exports also
+require that they still be an admin. Export archives are deleted after expiry.
+
+An account ZIP can be uploaded at `/account/imports/new` by any signed-in
+identity, including one with no account memberships. Imports create a separate
+account and do not merge into or modify existing accounts. The importer becomes
+the new account's owner. People are matched by normalized email addresses, not
+by instance-specific identity IDs. No sign-in tokens, sessions, or passkeys are
+exported. Public board links and webhook secrets are regenerated, webhooks are
+disabled, and historical notification bundles are not sent again.
+
+## Archive format
+
+`manifest.json` identifies the archive kind (`account` or `cards`), format version,
+source account ID, and (for account archives) record/file counts. Account records
+are JSON files at `<table_name>/<original_id>.json`. Card JSON also includes its
+description and comments; canonical rich text records live under
+`action_text_rich_texts/`. All account attachment bytes, including pictures,
+embedded files, and avatars, are under `files/<original_blob_id>`. User records
+include their email addresses instead of identity IDs. Rich text uses portable
+attachment/mention references which are re-signed on import.
+
+Personal card exports use `cards/<card_number>/card.json`, with comments, HTML
+content, and an attachment list containing filenames and archive paths. Files
+are alongside each card. Other people's drafts, inaccessible boards, and other
+accounts are excluded. These archives are for reading, not account restoration.
+
+## Storage and failure behavior
+
+Exports use ZIP64-capable streaming. Disk exports use a temporary ZIP; S3 exports
+stream directly through multipart upload. Imports read archive ranges from the
+configured disk or S3 service. S3 attachment restoration also uses multipart
+streaming; disk restoration stages one attachment at a time. The whole ZIP is
+never loaded into memory. Archive metadata and individual JSON records have
+bounded sizes to reject malformed input (128 MiB and 16 MiB respectively).
+
+Imports remap UUIDs, account URL prefixes, and storage keys. Record references,
+account boundaries, counts, entry sizes, CRCs, and attachment checksums are
+validated. Database writes, search indexing, and storage accounting share a
+transaction. Failed imports roll it back and delete newly uploaded files; source
+ZIPs are deleted after either outcome. The requester receives an email with a
+link to the new account or a reason for failure.
+
+Run the regression coverage with:
+
+```sh
+PARALLEL_WORKERS=1 bin/rails test test/models/account/data_transfer \
+  test/models/account/import_test.rb test/models/export_test.rb \
+  test/models/zip_file_test.rb test/models/zip_file \
+  test/controllers/account/exports_controller_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/account/imports_controller_test.rb \
+  test/controllers/my/exports_controller_test.rb
+```
